## Audit cycle 64 — PR #664 inactive inverse-D cubic budget

| Field | Record |
| --- | --- |
| Audit base | `origin/main=edfceba1fa7743332071c0825660de0654113b6f`, state `2026-10-08.audit.125`; Target C C-II open |
| Source branch and immutable snapshot | `swarm/sol/inactive-diameter-cubic-moment-20261004` at `e62f84e51590fa2660439ea0a2374e4a19e94c5c`; PR #664 open, 3 branch-only / 314 main-only |
| Source artifacts | `research/target_c_inactive_diameter_cubic_moment_2026-10-04.md`, SHA-256 `cec18bd5167f82f513274345262309c11b45f7139ee2892f7d0b228909e42c19`; `research/verify_target_c_inactive_diameter_cubic_moment_2026_10_04.py`, SHA-256 `0aaa48bcfda8e03cfb6fb156906fc6c8082dc6df505d81404d508d3d388761aa` |
| Exact claims audited | Finite all-source deficit-weighted cubic budget for inverse-D pairs, its global truncation, square-root matching for every linear near-diameter subgraph, the stronger distinct-partner bound for one-pair-per-owner packets, the separate bounded-degree near-unit matching fact, and the all-vertex near-diameter phase-purity estimate. An explicit nonglobal family has a linear inactive near-diameter star but actual diameter degree one. |
| Independent reconstruction | A maximal diameter-D convex completion has every boundary point incident to a diameter, and the planar constant-width characterization gives constant width D. Its curvature measure has density in [0,D], and the support-area quadratic form is positive semidefinite on antiperiodic H1. Near-diameter neighbors have support slack at most c/D; sublinearity controls the full angular interval, unit separation gives span at least (d-1)/(2D), and radius-2sqrt(c) support caps have overlap at most M_c including both antipodal lifts. The cutoff cosine test pays the nonzero curvature error; heavy vertices contribute at least D(tan(a)-a). Summing their interval functions, using overlap and the area-deficit Cauchy bound, yields the cubic moment with constant 3072M_c. Holder on a maximum-matching endpoint cover then gives the stated packet constant. The one-vertex version gives phase purity. |
| Dependencies and scope | Only the global truncation, global packet result, and global phase-purity corollary use canonical `C-FS-GEOM`; the finite deficit-weighted inequality is for every finite planar unit-separated source. KMW Theorem 0.1 is not used for arbitrary-set completion: its formal statement assumes Hamiltonicity. Completion was reconstructed by Zorn and the planar boundary-diameter characterization (KMW, Introduction/Proposition 1.3), with KMW's p.421 proof separately noting Eggleston Theorem 54. All source vertices, including contact isolates, are retained. |
| Counterexamples and corrections checked | Three exact rational nonglobal fixtures show the inactive root-degree separation. The analytic family for every integer D>=64 was checked from its rational parametrization: neighbors are pairwise >1, all pair distances are <=D with one actual diameter neighbor at the root, and a collinear n-point competitor has diameter 2floor(D/5)+1<D. Thus this is not a counterexample to any global-minimizer conclusion. Near-star intervals are not treated as disjoint; antipodal cap overlap is paid twice; near-unit pairs do not become KKT contacts; phase purity requires at least two H_c-neighbors. |
