| Field | Record |
| --- | --- |
| Exact claim audited | A contact-minimal global minimizer with a nonisolated contact vertex cannot admit a finite unit-separated packet with one extra strictly protected site beyond an explicit deletion set, because deleting the vertex and replacing the deletion set loses contact edges and creates none. |
| Independent reconstruction | Let `R` be the replacement deletion set, `t` old contacts incident to `R`, and `j` every new unit pair touching the packet. If `j<t`, global minimality handles a strict diameter decrease, while contact minimality handles diameter `D`; the latter uses no diameter-pair minimum. For a one-spare packet choose `R=S` if `S` contains a nonisolated contact vertex, otherwise `R=S union {v}`. Strict lower clearance gives `j=0<t`. New-involving pairs at exactly `D` are harmless in this contact-loss case. |
| Corrected scope | Refine `C-CONTACT-BUDGET-DIAMETER-EXCHANGE-ENDPOINT`: its `j<t` branch needs only contact minimality and permits new-involving distances `<=D`; its `j=t,d>0` branch still needs diameter-pair minimality and strict upper slack. If `S` already contains a nonisolated contact vertex, no spare site is needed. |
| Adversarial checks | Checked both placements of the deleted nonisolated vertex, `S` empty, exact `D` new pairs, retained contact isolates, and triangle-freeness. With strict new lower gaps, packet points are contact-isolated and the retained graph is induced; no source point is omitted except an explicitly deleted label. No wall capacity is inferred. |
| Verifier boundary | The source report states no computation and contains no verifier. This finite counting argument is analytic; no unrelated script was treated as proof evidence. |
| Verdict and canonical disposition | PASS — update the scope and audit pointer for `C-CONTACT-BUDGET-DIAMETER-EXCHANGE-ENDPOINT`. The one-spare-site statement is a direct corollary, so add no duplicate theorem ID. Do not promote packet existence, residual-cage composition, or wall-specific extraction. |
